{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And he encountered the place, and lodged there, because the sun had set.</b> Our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Ḥullin 91a, see there) that this is Mount Moriah, of which it is said, ״וַיַּרְא אֶת הַמָּקוֹם מֵרָחוֹק״ (Genesis 22:4). He called that place simply 'the place,' because every place has a name attached to it — either after its owners or after the nature of the place, for by this it is distinguished from other places. But this place — its nature and its name were hidden, as explained above in the perasha of וַיֵּרָא on the verse ״ה׳ יִרְאֶה״ (Genesis 22:14); therefore he called it simply 'the place,' for it did not yet possess the name by which it would be distinguished from other places." "Another matter: <b>Therefore he called it simply 'the place,'</b> because it is the place of the world — both in that there is the Foundation Stone there and from it the world was founded, and in that the whole world still rests upon it, for from it the influx goes forth to the entire world. And Jacob sensed that this would be the place of the Temple, for he saw that the sun had set upon him out of its season, because this holy place dulls the wheel of the sun and has no need for the sun — and on the contrary, the sun needs it, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Numbers Rabbah 15:2): Rabbi Avin said, you find that one who wishes to make windows for himself makes them wide on the inside and narrow on the outside, so that they may draw in light from outside; but the windows of the Temple were wide on the outside and narrow on the inside — why? So that the light would go out from the Temple and illuminate the world, as it says (Ezekiel 43:2), ״וְהָאָרֶץ הֵאִירָה מִכְּבוֹדוֹ״, and it is written (Jeremiah 17:12), ״כִּסֵּא כָבוֹד מָרוֹם מֵרִאשׁוֹן מְקוֹם מִקְדָּשֵׁנוּ״. See this version in the Yalkut, perasha of תְּצַוֶּה. From this there is proof that the sun does not illuminate the Temple; therefore the light of the sun dimmed immediately upon entering the holy place, ״וְהָיָה לְעֵת עֶרֶב יִהְיֶה אוֹר״ (Zechariah 14:7), for HaShem illuminated the supernal light from this holy place, until HaShem appeared to him in a vision on that night." "And there is yet another proper allusion, in what they said in the Midrash Yalkut, that the sun set two hours before its time. And why specifically two hours? Rather, it alluded here to the future: thus will the sun of Israel set two years before its time, as Rashi explained in the perasha of וָאֶתְחַנַּן (Deuteronomy 4:25), that HaShem hastened the exile two years before the appointed time, as it says (Daniel 9:14), ״וַיִּשְׁקֹד ה׳ עַל הָרָעָה״. And if so, the Temple that will be built in this place will ultimately be destroyed two years before the time of its setting; therefore the allusion came to Jacob through this sunset, that there would be a sign placed in his hand that in the future the Temple would be destroyed two years before its time. Therefore ״וַיִּקַּח מֵאַבְנֵי הַמָּקוֹם וַיָּשֶׂם מְרַאֲשֹׁתָיו״, and they said in the Midrash (cited by Rema, O.C. 559:2) that the pious and men of deed have the custom on the night of the Ninth of Av to place a stone under the head, and they said that they have support from this verse, ״וַיִּקַּח מֵאַבְנֵי הַמָּקוֹם״ etc., for Jacob saw the destruction. And who told the author of this Midrash that Jacob saw the destruction? Rather, he certainly derived this from the setting of the sun two hours early, as stated above." "<b>And he took from the stones of the place and set them at his head, etc.</b> After it became known to him that this place would be the House of Elohim that dulls the wheel of the sun, therefore he took from the stones of the place and set them as a guard for his head, to show the love of holiness that rested upon them, as it is written (Psalms 102:14), ״כִּי רָצוּ עֲבָדֶיךָ אֶת אֲבָנֶיהָ״. And he took twelve stones, corresponding to the twelve tribes, as is found in the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 68:11), and the stones quarreled, each one saying, 'Upon me let the righteous one lay his head,' until they became one stone — an allusion to the future, in the manner that the Moreh ha-Nevukhim wrote, that for this reason the Holy One, blessed be He, hid this place — that it would be the place of the Temple and the palace of the King — so that there would be no quarrel among the tribes, for every tribe would desire that holy place. And for the sake of peace it is said (Deuteronomy 12:5), ״אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳ מִכָּל שִׁבְטֵיכֶם״, and it is written (ibid. v. 14), ״כִּי אִם בַּמָּקוֹם אֲשֶׁר יִבְחַר ה׳ בְּאַחַד שְׁבָטֶיךָ״. How so? Rather, at the time that David acquired the threshing floor from Aravnah the Jebusite, he collected the silver from all the tribes, and so Rashi explained there. And if so, this quarrel that was among the stones and which ultimately became one stone — this was a sign and wonder concerning the future: thus there would be a quarrel among the crown-jewels, the twelve tribes of YHWH, for every tribe would say, 'Upon me let the Righteous One of the world lay His head' — this is the Temple, of which it says, ״מָרוֹם מֵרִאשׁוֹן מְקוֹם מִקְדָּשֵׁנוּ״ (Jeremiah 17:12). And ultimately they became one stone, through the fact that David collected the gold from all of them. And concerning this stone it is said of the Second Temple, ״וְהוֹצִיא אֶת הָאֶבֶן הָרֹאשָׁה״ (Zechariah 4:7) — meaning that stone which was at Jacob's head: he brought it forth to found with it the sanctuary of HaShem. And Jacob made of it a bed upon which to lie, as a sign that this would be the bed of שְׁלֹמֹה — the King to whom peace belongs, as Rashi explained in Song of Songs on the verse ״הִנֵּה מִטָּתוֹ שֶׁלִּשְׁלֹמֹה״ (Song of Songs 3:7)." "<b>And he lay down in that place.</b> This implies that beforehand he had not lain on a bed, for he did not wish to ascend the couch of his pallet until he found the place of the bed of שְׁלֹמֹה. And so David vowed, as it is said (Psalms 132:2–5), ״אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּע לַה׳, נָדַר לַאֲבִיר יַעֲקֹב: אִם אָבֹא בְּאֹהֶל בֵּיתִי, אִם אֶעֱלֶה עַל עֶרֶשׂ יְצוּעָי, אִם אֶתֵּן שְׁנַת לְעֵינָי, לְעַפְעַפַּי תְּנוּמָה, עַד אֶמְצָא מָקוֹם לַה׳, מִשְׁכָּנוֹת לַאֲבִיר יַעֲקֹב״. He mentioned Jacob always, for he too likewise vowed such a vow, as indicated by his saying ״וַיִּשְׁכַּב בַּמָּקוֹם הַהוּא״ — but not beforehand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {2 "The phrase 'מביאו רמ״א או״ח תקנה ב' indicates that this Midrash is cited by the Rema in Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayyim 559:2; resolved as a citation reference rather than a Midrash title.", 3 "Resolved 'הרב המורה' as the Moreh ha-Nevukhim (Guide for the Perplexed) by the Rambam."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:08.729129270Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}